meta {
  name: Get Tenant Branding (Public)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/public/tenant
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("identifies the host as platform or agency", function () {
    expect(["platform", "agency"]).to.include(res.getBody().kind);
  });

  test("always names something renderable", function () {
    // The whole point of the fallback chain: no caller should ever have to
    // render an empty wordmark.
    expect(res.getBody().name).to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /public/tenant

  What the SPA boots from: which agency (if any) owns the hostname this request
  arrived on, and what to render as its identity.

  **Module** — none. **Permission** — none, `@Public()`. It has to work before
  anyone has signed in, because the login page itself is branded.

  **How the tenant is chosen** — from the request's own `Host` header, resolved
  against the `agencyDomains` collection by `HostTenantResolver`. There is no
  parameter; to test a tenant locally, send a `Host` header or map the name in
  `/etc/hosts` (see below).

  **Response `200`**
  ```json
  {
    "kind": "agency",
    "agencyId": "...",
    "name": "Texas Holdings",
    "tagline": "Agency Portal",
    "logoUrl": "/api/v1/public/tenant/logo?variant=light",
    "logoDarkUrl": "/api/v1/public/tenant/logo?variant=dark",
    "faviconUrl": "/api/v1/public/tenant/favicon"
  }
  ```

  On the platform host the same shape comes back with `kind: "platform"`,
  `agencyId: null`, `name: "AgencyOps"` and every URL `null` — the platform mark
  is a bundled asset in the web app, not an upload.

  The URLs are **paths on the requesting origin**, deliberately. Same-origin
  keeps every tenant domain out of the object storage CORS allow-list (a static
  Terraform list), and a stable path is cacheable where a presigned URL is not.

  **Errors**
  - `404` — the hostname belongs to no agency and is not the platform host. The
    same answer `HostTenantGuard` gives, so this cannot be used to enumerate
    tenants.

  ⚠ **The web client must treat this `404` as definitive**, not as a failed
  request. It originally fell back to the platform brand on *any* error, which
  rendered a convincing "AgencyOps" sign-in form on an address where every
  subsequent request is refused by `HostTenantGuard` — correct credentials, then
  an unexplained failure. `fetchTenantBranding` now separates a `404` (show
  "this address isn't set up", no form) from a transient network/5xx failure
  (keep the last known brand and a working form, so an outage does not block the
  page people use to report it).

  ## Testing a tenant host locally
  ```
  # /etc/hosts
  127.0.0.1  app.sfa.local texasholdings.sfa.local
  ```
  Set `PLATFORM_HOST=app.sfa.local`, then either browse the name directly or:
  ```bash
  curl -H 'Host: texasholdings.sfa.local' http://localhost:4000/api/v1/public/tenant
  ```
}
